---
import BrandArtwork from "../../components/BrandArtwork.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/ResourceLayout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Code + Work Are Now One Workspace — Modesto"
  description="How Modesto collapsed the gap between writing code with AI agents and actually shipping work. An argument for unified workspace design."
>
  <article class="article-shell">

    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="article-eyebrow">
        <a href="/articles" class="back mono">← Blog</a>
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: center;">
          <button type="button" id="download-pdf-btn" onclick="window.print()" class="mono" style="background: none; border: none; color: var(--faint); font-size: 9px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; cursor: pointer; transition: color 150ms ease;">Download PDF ↓</button>
          <span class="mono article-date">August 17, 2026</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="article-cover"><BrandArtwork /></div>
      <div class="article-header-copy">
        <span class="mono article-cat">Product</span>
        <h1>Code + Work are now<br />one workspace.</h1>
        <p class="article-lede">
          For years, software teams kept a hard boundary between "where you write code" and "where you manage work." That boundary was never a law. It was a limitation. Modesto removes it.
        </p>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <span class="mono">Modesto Team</span>
          <span class="meta-sep mono">·</span>
          <span class="mono">10 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        Every developer knows the context switch. You are mid-thought in your editor, the agent has just returned a diff that changes three files and surfaces a decision you have not made yet. You need to write a plan, log a decision, notify a teammate, or checkpoint your thinking. So you open a different tool. Your browser. Your project tracker. Slack. Notion. Linear. Something.
      </p>

      <p>
        By the time you get back, the thread is cold. The agent has no memory of what you were considering. You type your way back into the work. This costs minutes each time, and dozens of minutes each day. Across a team, it costs cohesion.
      </p>

      <h2>The gap was always artificial</h2>

      <p>
        The separation of "coding tools" from "work management tools" made sense when code was written entirely by hand, one keystroke at a time, and work was tracked in physical rooms on physical boards. The two activities were genuinely different in pace, texture, and who was doing them.
      </p>

      <p>
        AI coding agents change that. When a tool like Codex or Claude Code can take a well-formed task and return a working implementation in seconds, the bottleneck shifts. It is no longer the implementation — it is the specification, the decision-making, and the handoff. The work around the code becomes the expensive part.
      </p>

      <p>
        That work around the code has always existed. Engineers write plans before writing code. They document decisions after writing it. They review each other's diffs, leave comments, ask questions, assign follow-ups. These activities require thought, judgment, and communication. They are not administrative overhead. They are the actual substance of building software with other people.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        The gap between "coding tool" and "work tool" was never a feature. It was an accident of how the industry grew.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>What a unified workspace actually looks like</h2>

      <p>
        When we designed Modesto, we started from a specific question: what does a developer actually need to move a piece of work from idea to merged commit, using one or more AI coding agents, without losing the thread?
      </p>

      <p>
        The answer was not "a better IDE plugin." It was not "an AI layer on top of Linear." It was a dedicated desktop application that treats the agent session and the work context as the same thing — because they are.
      </p>

      <p>
        In Modesto, a session is not just a chat window to an AI. It is a workspace that carries:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>The agent you are working with (Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, Gemini, or any CLI-compatible model)</li>
        <li>The project it is operating on</li>
        <li>The task it is working toward</li>
        <li>The checkpoints it has reached — diffs, decisions, and notes you can read back and hand off</li>
        <li>The full context of the conversation that led to the current state of the code</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        When you checkpoint a session, you are not just saving a git commit. You are saving a structured record of what the agent understood, what you decided, and what comes next. That record can be handed to another agent, another developer, or picked up by you three days later without re-establishing context from scratch.
      </p>

      <h2>The checkpoint is the handoff</h2>

      <p>
        In traditional software workflows, the handoff is an event. Someone finishes a task, writes a summary, assigns it, and moves on. The person receiving the work has to reconstruct the context from whatever documentation exists — which is usually incomplete, because documentation is written after the fact by people who have already moved on mentally.
      </p>

      <p>
        With AI coding agents, the context lives in the session. The conversation history, the decisions made along the way, the rejected approaches — all of it is present if you design the system to preserve it. Modesto does. Every checkpoint is a structured snapshot that includes the agent's current understanding, the outstanding decisions, and the next logical step.
      </p>

      <p>
        This means a handoff from one agent to another — or from an agent to a human reviewer — does not require translation. The context is already in a form the next person or system can act on. You do not need to write a summary. You hand over the checkpoint.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        A checkpoint is not a save point. It is a complete unit of work — context included — that can be resumed by anyone, anywhere.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why local-first matters for this</h2>

      <p>
        A unified workspace only works if you can trust it with everything that goes through it. If your plans, your decisions, your agent sessions, and your code are all in one place, that place has to be somewhere you control.
      </p>

      <p>
        Modesto runs entirely on your machine. There is no cloud session broker, no prompt logging service, no third-party server that receives your code while the agent is working. The AI providers you connect to (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and others) receive only the prompts you send them — which is what you opted into when you chose those providers. Modesto itself sees nothing.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not a privacy feature added to a product that was designed for the cloud. It is an architectural constraint that shaped every decision we made. When your workspace is local, you can store sensitive context in it, work in it without a network connection, and share it selectively with teammates through standard git tooling.
      </p>

      <h2>One session, many agents</h2>

      <p>
        The shift to unified workspaces also changes how you work with multiple AI models. Today, most developers pick one assistant and commit to it. This is a false constraint.
      </p>

      <p>
        Different models are good at different things. One might produce cleaner diffs on well-specified tasks. Another might reason better through ambiguous architecture decisions. A third might be faster and cheaper for mechanical work like writing tests or renaming symbols. The best development environment lets you route work to the right model for the job without duplicating context.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because Modesto's checkpoints are model-agnostic — structured around the work rather than around any specific agent's format — you can start a session with one model and continue it with another. The context travels with the task, not with the tool.
      </p>

      <h2>What this means for teams</h2>

      <p>
        The implications for team software development are significant. When individual developers work in isolated silos — their own IDE, their own chat history with their own model — the knowledge generated during development does not accumulate at the team level. It evaporates when the session ends.
      </p>

      <p>
        A workspace designed around persistent, shareable checkpoints changes this. Decisions made during development are recorded in a form that is useful for review, documentation, and future work. The history of how the code came to be is not lost when the developer closes their laptop.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is what we mean when we say Modesto is built for teams, not just individuals. The workspace is designed to produce artifacts — checkpoints, plans, diffs, decisions — that are worth preserving and sharing. Not as a side effect. As the primary output.
      </p>

      <h2>The model is already obvious</h2>

      <p>
        There is nothing radical about combining code and work into one place. The most mature engineering cultures have always treated these as inseparable. The best engineers write plans before they write code. They document decisions as they make them. They review work carefully because they understand that the cost of a bad decision compounds.
      </p>

      <p>
        What AI coding agents do is accelerate the implementation so much that the surrounding work — the planning, the deciding, the reviewing, the handing off — becomes the visible constraint. The bottleneck moves. When it does, the tools have to move with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Modesto is our answer to where the tools should land.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <footer class="article-footer">
      <div class="article-footer-left">
        <span class="mono">Modesto · August 2026</span>
        <p>Modesto is available now for macOS, Windows, and Linux. Local-first, model-agnostic, built for how software teams actually work with AI agents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="article-footer-right">
        <a href="/articles" class="mono back-link">← All articles</a>
      </div>
    </footer>

  </article>

  <!-- Print-only branded footer -->
  <div class="article-print-footer" aria-hidden="true">
    <img src="/modesto-logo.svg" alt="" />
    <span>Modesto</span>
    <span class="article-print-footer-url">modesto.dev/articles</span>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article-print-footer {
    display: none;
  }

  .article-shell {
    width: min(100% - 48px, 1440px);
    margin: 0 auto;
    border-inline: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  .article-header {
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 20px clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .back {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .article-date {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-cover {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 6;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-cover img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .article-header-copy {
    padding: clamp(40px, 7vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 45% at 50% -10%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--violet) 10%, transparent), transparent 68%);
  }

  .article-cat {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
    line-height: 0.88;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .article-lede {
    max-width: 600px;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .meta-sep {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* ── Rule ── */
  .article-rule {
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Body ── */
  .article-body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr min(680px, 100%) 1fr;
    padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 100px) 0;
    row-gap: 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-body > * {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: 64px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .article-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 28px 0;
    padding-left: 24px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .article-body li {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body blockquote {
    margin: 48px 0;
    padding: 32px 36px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper-deep) 60%, transparent);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* ── Footer ── */
  .article-footer {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: end;
    padding: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-footer-left {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .article-footer-left > .mono {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-footer-left p {
    max-width: 480px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .article-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    width: fit-content;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .article-cta:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .article-shell {
      width: min(100% - 32px, 1440px);
    }

    .article-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      padding-inline: clamp(20px, 5vw, 40px);
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .article-footer {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  /* ── Print / "Download PDF" ──
     No PDF library — reuses the browser's own print-to-PDF against the page
     content already rendered. Keeps the real theme colors/gradients instead
     of flattening to plain black-on-white, hides site chrome (nav, the ←
     Blog/Download PDF/date bar, this article's own closing footer, and the
     global site footer) so only the cover image through the end of the
     article prints, and shows a small branded footer in their place. */
  @media print {
    * {
      -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
      print-color-adjust: exact;
      color-adjust: exact;
    }

    .site-header,
    .global-footer,
    .footer-mark,
    .capability-ticker,
    .article-eyebrow,
    .article-footer {
      display: none !important;
    }

    body {
      background: #ffffff;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-shell {
      width: 100%;
      border-inline: 0;
    }

    /* Safari's print engine is well documented to mis-render CSS Grid (and
       occasionally flex) during the print pass — tracks can collapse or
       children can lose their placement, which is what "ignores all our
       styling" looks like from the outside even though the colors/fonts
       below are applied correctly. Every layout still visible in print gets
       forced to plain block flow here so there's no grid/flex left for
       Safari to get wrong; Chrome/Firefox render identically either way. */
    body,
    .article-body,
    .article-body p,
    .article-body li,
    .article-header,
    .article-header-copy {
      font-family: "DM Sans", -apple-system, "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif;
    }

    .mono,
    .article-meta,
    .article-print-footer {
      font-family: "DM Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    }

    .article-header {
      display: block;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-header-copy {
      display: block;
    }

    .article-cover,
    .article-header-copy {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-lede,
    .article-meta {
      color: #4a4d57;
    }

    .article-body {
      display: block;
      width: min(680px, 100%);
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding-bottom: 0;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: unset;
    }

    .article-body p {
      color: #2b2d33;
      orphans: 3;
      widows: 3;
    }

    .article-body h2 {
      color: #14151a;
      break-after: avoid;
      page-break-after: avoid;
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body li {
      color: #2b2d33;
    }

    .article-body blockquote,
    .article-body ul,
    .article-body img {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body blockquote {
      background: #f4f4f6;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-body code {
      background: #f0f0f2;
      border-color: #ddd;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    /* Normal document flow, not position:fixed — a fixed-position footer
       gets painted at the same screen coordinate on every printed page and
       can overlap the last line or two of that page's content instead of
       repeating cleanly, which is what was making text unreadable near page
       breaks. This renders once, after the article ends. */
    .article-print-footer {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      height: 34px;
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding: 0 4px;
      border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
      color: #6b6e78;
      font: 500 10px "DM Sans", sans-serif;
    }

    .article-print-footer img {
      width: 14px;
      height: 14px;
      object-fit: contain;
    }

    .article-print-footer span:first-of-type {
      color: #14151a;
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .article-print-footer-url {
      margin-left: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
